There is no SvelteKit `isr` switch for the Cloudflare adapter. [That configuration belongs to adapter-vercel](https://svelte.dev/docs/kit/adapter-vercel).

[Vercel ISR](https://vercel.com/docs/incremental-static-regeneration) stores generated content and regenerates it. [Kit #661](https://github.com/sveltejs/kit/issues/661) asks for a general regeneration feature.

Cloudflare can still cache rendered pages.

[Workers Cache](https://blog.cloudflare.com/workers-cache/) puts a cache in front of a Worker entrypoint. Its [stale-while-revalidate behavior](https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/cache/) serves a stale response while the Worker refreshes it.

## What our bake-off proves

We compare three local candidates:

- A static-assets gateway.
- A SvelteKit prerender gateway.
- A Workers Cache entrypoint.

The cache candidate mints a marker inside the rendering entrypoint. Repeated identical requests do not reuse it.

An assets response carries `CF-Cache-Status: HIT`. That proves only asset-cache reuse. The selected Miniflare implementation does not implement Workers Cache handling.

The cache candidate's home-page Markdown timings are:

| Request | Local time |
| --- | --- |
| Cold: first application request in a fresh runtime | 8.25 ms |
| Warm: median of 20 sequential requests | 1.69 ms |

Neither number measures an edge cache HIT.

The check does not prove:

- Stale-while-revalidate.
- Purge.
- Representation isolation across Workers Cache HITs.

The accepted configuration leaves cache behavior unproven.

## What we choose

We choose [static assets](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/commit/a337a96). [`apps/mischief/src/static-assets.ts`](https://github.com/joelhooks/rat-stack/blob/main/apps/mischief/src/static-assets.ts) serves separate HTML and Markdown assets. The dynamic signup page stays outside that path.

See [Worker init runs twice](/lore/init-runs-twice) for another boundary a local check can miss.
